1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,575
AI tools run on data,
including whatever you type into them.

2
00:00:04,575 --> 00:00:07,226
This section is about
what's safe to share,

3
00:00:07,226 --> 00:00:11,060
what never is, and where to
find good data for practice.

4
00:00:11,660 --> 00:00:15,718
When you type into a public AI tool,
your words leave your device.

5
00:00:15,718 --> 00:00:19,413
Depending on the tool and your
settings, they may be stored,

6
00:00:19,413 --> 00:00:22,683
used to improve the tool,
or read by human reviewers.

7
00:00:22,683 --> 00:00:28,050
For example, Google says reviewed Gemini
chats can be kept for up to three years.

8
00:00:28,650 --> 00:00:33,814
Here's a simple rule: think of a traffic
light. Green means go: public statistics,

9
00:00:33,814 --> 00:00:36,925
published reports,
and made-up practice examples.

10
00:00:36,925 --> 00:00:39,849
Yellow means check first:
internal information

11
00:00:39,849 --> 00:00:42,960
that isn't about patients,
like a draft schedule.

12
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:45,946
Red means never:
anything about a real patient,

13
00:00:45,946 --> 00:00:49,830
other organizations' confidential files,
and passwords.

14
00:00:50,430 --> 00:00:52,347
Removing names isn't enough.

15
00:00:52,347 --> 00:00:57,369
One famous study estimated that 87
percent of Americans were likely unique,

16
00:00:57,369 --> 00:01:01,140
based on just their ZIP code,
birth date, and sex.

17
00:01:01,740 --> 00:01:05,025
In small rural communities,
the risk is even higher,

18
00:01:05,025 --> 00:01:07,443
because fewer people share any detail.

19
00:01:07,443 --> 00:01:12,278
That's why, under the federal rule for
removing identifiers from health data,

20
00:01:12,278 --> 00:01:15,501
only the first three digits
of a ZIP code may stay,

21
00:01:15,501 --> 00:01:18,663
and only if that area has
more than 20,000 people.

22
00:01:18,663 --> 00:01:23,030
And it's why the CDC's public
data tool hides counts below ten.

23
00:01:23,630 --> 00:01:26,686
So where can you practice?
With public data.

24
00:01:26,686 --> 00:01:31,846
For example, CDC PLACES has local health
estimates for nearly every county.

25
00:01:31,846 --> 00:01:35,920
The USDA's rural-urban codes
show how rural each county is.

26
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:40,530
And HRSA's data warehouse maps
areas short of health workers.

27
00:01:41,130 --> 00:01:45,577
When you need examples that look like
patient records, use made-up ones.

28
00:01:45,577 --> 00:01:48,927
Write your own,
with obviously fake names and details.

29
00:01:48,927 --> 00:01:51,668
Or use free synthetic data,
such as Synthea,

30
00:01:51,668 --> 00:01:55,540
which generates realistic but
fictional patient records.

31
00:01:56,140 --> 00:01:59,828
Before you paste anything into
an AI tool, ask three questions.

32
00:01:59,828 --> 00:02:04,150
Is it public, or made up? Could it
identify a person, even without a name?

33
00:02:04,150 --> 00:02:06,916
Would I be comfortable
if someone else read it?

34
00:02:06,916 --> 00:02:09,390
If you're not sure, don't paste it.

35
00:02:09,990 --> 00:02:14,315
Public, summary, or made-up data:
yes. Real patient records: never.

36
00:02:14,315 --> 00:02:18,340
Next, you'll put this into
practice with a real AI tool.
